with the disney plus debut of the falcon  and the winter soldier  sam wilson is set to take on a leading  role in the mcu  so there's no better time to find out a  little more about his comic book history  this is the untold truth of the falcon  the falcon first debuted in captain  america number 117  which was published all the way back in  1969. in this comic  sam wilson is a resident of harlem who  discovers that a group of hydra agents  led by the red skull are operating out  of a tropical island called exile  wilson and his trained falcon redwing  soon discover the group's nefarious plan  and along the way he learns that they've  been enslaving indigenous tribes on the  island  sam is then encouraged by captain  america himself  to adopt a superhero moniker of his own  and save the islanders  from the red skull's men artist gene  colen  had the idea for the falcon partly as a  response to the civil rights movement  and for a number of reasons he's  generally regarded as one of the more  empowering  characters of the silver age of comics  for one  he's arguably the first mainstream black  hero to not  actually have the word black in his name  falcon was also considered an equal to  captain america  rather than a sidekick and the two  famously received co-billing in the  ongoing captain america series for  almost 100 issues  despite receiving co-billing with cap  however it took a long time for falcon  to get a chance to shine in his own  series  indeed his origin story has been  retconned several times  and like many other comic book heroes  he's bounced between  several different teams over the years  falcon finally appeared as a solo act in  a 1979 issue of  marvel premiere a tryout series that the  company used to gauge  interest in new material although it  didn't lead to much  four years later he led a four-issue  mini-series that identified sam wilson  as a mutant  but that plot twist was wisely retconned  in 2001.  it wasn't until 2017 almost 50 years  after the character's debut  that the falcon got his own true solo  series the sensibly titled falcon  led by writer rodney barnes and artist  joshua casera  however as marvel fans will know wilson  led his own series a few years before  that  but it wasn't as the falcon at all  starting in 2014  sam wilson replaced his original partner  steve rogers as captain america  the transition occurs after rogers has  the super  soldier serum drained from his body  during a battle with iron nail  which forces him to hang up the shield  as he rapidly ages into an old man  considering how long the two had fought  side by side  wilson was a natural fit to take over  the role and continue  captain america's legacy this makes the  ending of avengers in-game even more  significant to longtime fans of the  comics  since both bucky barnes and sam wilson  have taken up the shield in the comics  it really could have been either of them  that filled steve rogers shoes  but while rogers and wilson do share a  knack for heroism  it will be interesting to see how the  falcon's totally different backstory  meshes with the role of captain america  in the mcu  for his mcu appearances falcon was given  a handful of technological  upgrades that fit with a more modern  interpretation of the character  in his original iterations however he  was pretty much just a dude in a costume  as his character started to gain a bit  more traction  certain elements were added for example  miniature solar arrays were inlaid into  the fiber  which allowed his suit to easily provide  its own power  in both the movies and later iterations  of the comics  falcon pilots a stark industry's drone  codenamed  redwing payload's secure  thanks sam don't thank me i'm not  thinking that thing  his name is red wing i'm still not  thinking he's cute  that's a far cry from his early years  though when he was partnered with an  actual trained falcon named redwing  falcon's origin  involves him forging a connection with  the bird who's been with the character  ever since that first issue of captain  america  and the two share a bond that goes far  beyond the emotional  it seems only right then that red wing  stays by falcon's side even when he  takes up captain america's shield  falcon has gone through a handful of  other technological upgrades over the  years too  his first modern costume was created at  captain america's request  not long after he made his debut and it  was even made by a familiar face  king t'challa of wakanda originally  black panther designed falcons wings to  have just a few basic features  including the solar receptors jet  boosters and a small handful of other  tricks and gizmos  over time however falcon has had  t'challa and a few others  upgrade his suit to give it the  versatility and power to rival marvel's  slickest heroes  one more recent iteration of the costume  features holographic wings made of hard  light  which feature a maximum wingspan of 50  feet  the wings can shield him from enemy  firepower they're virtually  indestructible  and they can be manipulated into a  variety of shapes and configurations at  the drop of a hat  all of this is controlled through a  cybernetic connection run through  falcon's mask  directly into his brain meaning the  whole system is controlled by his  thoughts  quite a leap from wings made of fabric  receipt for your gear  bird costume come on i didn't write it  falcon's origin story has been changed a  few different times  but one thing has remained constant he  was originally a relatively ordinary man  with no superpowers to speak of  still when he fights alongside redwing  in his debut story  the connection between wilson and his  avian ally seems abnormally strong  but it later turns out that the red  skull himself  helped give this power to wilson as part  of a plot that revolved around a very  familiar marvel macguffin  the cosmic cube in a later story it's  revealed that the red skull  used the cosmic cube to brainwash wilson  into turning against captain america  an act which also established a  telepathic link between the falcon and  redwing  as he grows into his role as falcon  wilson learns to expand this  link and what begins merely as a means  of communication  soon becomes the ability to see through  red wings eyes  eventually wilson becomes able to see  through any bird's eyes  and even access their memories but the  falcons encounter with the cosmic cube  had other side effects as well  one of which would lead to a very  unfortunate retcon  the confusing nature of comic books can  mean some characters are given many  backstories  since so many different writers are  involved in such a wide shared universe  some pretty strange contradictions and  retcons can emerge if you go back  far enough and despite falcon enjoying a  pretty strong debut  his character was later given a new and  immensely disappointing  origin in his original story falcon is a  social worker from harlem  that background was overturned in a  later run however in which it is  revealed that sam wilson's true  identity is snap wilson a harlem mobster  in pimp  who is essentially the epitome of an  ugly racial stereotype  his social worker origen is explained to  have been a false memory  implanted in wilson's mind as a side  effect of the cosmic cube's powers  fast forward a few decades to 2015 and  it turns out that the snap  wilson origin was the actual false  memory  implanted by red skull to so confusion  in the falcon's mind  wilson's life as a social worker is  subsequently revealed to be the real  memory  having simply been overwritten by the  power of the cube  comics love to critique satirize and  subvert  things that are happening in the real  world and falcon has tackled his fair  share of real world issues too  one of the strangest instances of this  occurred during a brief avengers run  that began in 1979  when wilson joined the team and quit  almost immediately  at this point in time the united nations  liaison for the avengers is a man  named henry geyrich who tasks captain  america with recruiting falcon to join  the team  however falcon learns that guy rich has  merely tapped him to fill a racial  hiring quota  and he isn't best pleased falcon has  come and gone from a variety of teams  over the years  but he has almost always stayed true to  his convictions  as a character he believes in being  recognized for a person's skills and  abilities  and his principle in quitting a group  like the avengers due to being a token  hire  epitomizes that belief in many ways sam  wilson was the one who helped steve  rogers heal from the grief of losing his  old partner  bucky barnes when it is revealed that  bucky has been alive all along  albeit having been brainwashed into  becoming a soviet assassin  wilson is there for rogers as they try  to help barnes regain his memories their  dynamic in the mcu  is a little different than in the comics  as in the movies  the falcon and captain america don't  have half a century's worth of history  together  in the comics however a mutual respect  is earned between  sam and bucky not just because of their  relationship with cap  but because they both spent time  carrying the shield when roger stepped  aside  there weren't many story lines that  really explored this relationship  properly though until the 2020 falcon  and winter soldier series that is  when the series picks up falcon is back  in the bird suit and bucky has received  a pardon for his murderous past but is  contingent on working for an agency that  locates  and eliminates domestic terrorists the  series forces  wilson and barnes together as they try  to track down  baron zemo as well as a handful of other  villains including the natural  a psychotic teenage assassin with a  captain america fetish  over time the series deepens their  friendship and allows both to evolve as  heroes  much like the upcoming disney plus  series as poised do  not too much is known about the disney  plus falcon and winter soldier series so  far  as marvel and disney have been typically  tight-lipped about what to expect  but one or two details are certain at  least oh yeah i can see it working  gear's turning oh they're malfunctioning  they're on fire  most importantly both anthony mackie and  sebastian  stan will be continuing in their  respective roles of sam wilson and bucky  barnes  joined by returning mcu stars emily van  camp  who plays sharon carter and daniel  brewell as the nefarious baron zemo  joining the cast is wyatt russell who is  taking on the role of john walker  the u.s government approved replacement  for steve rogers  with wilson having been handed captain  america shield at the end of avengers  end game  it seems certain that the show will  focus on wilson's transition from the  falcon to the new captain america  the series was created and written by  malcolm spellman who is best known for  writing several episodes of the tv  series  empire very few details are known about  the falcon in the winter soldiers plot  but it isn't all that hard to figure out  which comic books have inspired the  series story  especially considering where the  characters ended up after avengers end  game  the most likely route for the show to  take would involve borrowing beats from  the captain america sam  wilson run in 2015 captain america  fought that battle with the iron nail  which led to his rapid aging and  subsequent retirement from his life as a  superhero  rogers then passes the mantle to sam  wilson who has to cope with the  responsibilities of becoming captain  america  in light of the similar circumstances at  the conclusion of endgame  this storyline seems a likely influence  on the series  beyond that though anything could happen  but considering the involvement of so  many villains and anti-heroes  including baron zemo john walker batrack  the leaper  and flagsmasher you can be sure of at  least one thing  sam wilson and bucky barnes are gonna  have their hands full  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite marvel characters are  coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

